% ============================================================================
% tooth-parts — each STRUCTURAL PART of a tooth and what it is / its role,
% as the source states it (adj-facts-stdlib, ANATOMY).
% ============================================================================
% A tooth is not one solid lump — it is built out of named LAYERS and regions,
% and each part sits in a characteristic place and does a characteristic job.
% The crown is the part you can see; enamel is its outer surface; just beneath
% the enamel is dentin; cementum covers the root; the pulp in the middle holds
% the blood vessels and nerves; and the root anchors the tooth in the jawbone.
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? tooth_part_role(enamel, $Role)      % outer_surface
%
% This library is DISTINCT from anatomy/tooth-types, which maps a tooth TYPE
% (incisors, canines, molars) to the job it does. HERE the key is a tooth PART
% — a piece of ONE tooth's structure — and the value is what that part IS or
% where it sits, in the source's own words.
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `tooth_part_role(part, role)` carrying the table's citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the role
% WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on anything
% that is not one of these tooth parts (it never invents a role).
%
% The six structural parts and the role / description the source states for
% each, as a little truth table:
%
%     tooth part   role / what it is (source's word)   a beginner's cue
%     ----------   ---------------------------------   ------------------------
%     crown        visible_part                        the part of the tooth you can see
%     enamel       outer_surface                       the outer surface of the crown
%     dentin       beneath_enamel                      the layer just beneath the enamel
%     pulp         blood_vessels_and_nerves            the soft core with blood and nerves
%     cementum     covers_roots                        the calcified material over the root
%     root         extends_into_jawbone                the part anchored down in the jaw
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a role and recall which part it is:
%
%     ? tooth_part_role($Part, covers_roots)   % cementum
%
% A word that is NOT one of these tooth parts — `femur`, `gum`, `rock` — is
% deliberately NOT a row, so a tooth-part recall ABSTAINS on it rather than
% inventing a role. That honest-abstention property is what makes the table
% safe to build a reasoner on: it answers exactly the part→role pairs it can
% ground, and only those.
%
% The `role` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase atom, chosen to echo a
% phrase the source EXPLICITLY states for that part — never one the source does
% not support (the atom uses the source's own words, so every value stays
% literally checkable against the quoted span below).
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every part→role pair below
% was read out of a fetched NIH source this session, and any part whose role was
% not cleanly stated would have been dropped). Each pair, with the primary
% source it was copied from and the verbatim span that states it:
%
%   crown → visible_part
%     https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/002214.htm
%     (MedlinePlus Medical Encyclopedia — U.S. National Library of Medicine, NIH)
%     "The part of the tooth you can see is called the crown."
%
%   enamel → outer_surface
%     https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/002214.htm
%     (MedlinePlus Medical Encyclopedia — U.S. National Library of Medicine, NIH)
%     "The outer surface of the crown is made of enamel."
%
%   dentin → beneath_enamel
%     https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/002214.htm
%     (MedlinePlus Medical Encyclopedia — U.S. National Library of Medicine, NIH)
%     "Just beneath the enamel is dentin, a substance harder than bone."
%
%   pulp → blood_vessels_and_nerves
%     https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/002214.htm
%     (MedlinePlus Medical Encyclopedia — U.S. National Library of Medicine, NIH)
%     "The root contains blood vessels and nerves, which supply blood and
%      feeling to the whole tooth." ... "This area is known as the 'pulp' of the
%      tooth." (the pulp is the interior region holding the blood vessels and
%      nerves)
%
%   cementum → covers_roots
%     https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK538475/
%     (StatPearls, NCBI Bookshelf — U.S. National Library of Medicine, NIH)
%     "Cementoblasts form the cementum (calcified material covering the roots
%      of teeth)"
%
%   root → extends_into_jawbone
%     https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/002214.htm
%     (MedlinePlus Medical Encyclopedia — U.S. National Library of Medicine, NIH)
%     "The root of the tooth extends down into the jawbone."
%
% An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, so the `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold the single cleanest span: the MedlinePlus statement that
% fixes the enamel row (enamel → outer_surface). Every OTHER row's per-fact
% source and verbatim span is listed above, so each role remains independently
% checkable. Five of the six rows are MedlinePlus (a U.S. NLM/NIH consumer-
% health encyclopedia) and the sixth is StatPearls on the NCBI Bookshelf (also
% U.S. NLM/NIH-hosted, the same primary source anatomy/tooth-types cites) — both
% primary U.S. government (NIH) health resources, so `trust authoritative`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table tooth_part_role {
    columns part, role

    row (crown, visible_part)
    row (enamel, outer_surface)
    row (dentin, beneath_enamel)
    row (pulp, blood_vessels_and_nerves)
    row (cementum, covers_roots)
    row (root, extends_into_jawbone)

    source "The outer surface of the crown is made of enamel."
    locator "https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/002214.htm"
    trust authoritative
}
